eferred E4, which Sol played two turns later anyway. It was the first sign of a quiet slide in a game that had been dead even."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "The 20k's H4 turned Black's +11.8 into -5.2, a 17.0-point loss and the best chance White had all game. The move left the gap at F3 open, and the engine's own choice there was F3."}, {"turn": 20, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol answered with H3, not F3, and the lead swung from -5.2 to +14.2 for Black, a 19.4-point loss and the worst move of the game. The gift from turn 19 lasted exactly one move, and White never got near it again."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "G1 lost 13.1 points and took Black's lead from +13.5 to +26.6. Sol was now spending moves on the bottom edge, and from turn 22 on the engine's choice was C8, up on the left, every time."}, {"turn": 34, "text": "C7 lost 9.8 points, from +14.2 to +24.1, and the engine again wanted C8. The 20k took C8 on the very next move and built the row-8 wall over White's row-7 stones, which settled the board for good."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every command listed tools, read the player's own notes files, called mcp__go__play for each chosen move, and ran inspectLiberties from current-position-liberties.js to list existing chains and their liberties. The remaining scripts kept move logs and wrote the SGF and game notes. None of it generated, scored or ranked candidate moves or looked ahead.", "stop": false}}